UID:event-assignment-35215
DTSTART:20261001T110000Z
DTEND:20261001T110000Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Instructions\n------------\n\nAnswer all five problems and subm
 it your work as a single PDF document.\n\nYour answers must include all re
 quested calculations\, explanations\, R code and figures. When R code is r
 equested\, include the code in your PDF together with the requested numeri
 cal or graphical output.\n\nYour work will be reviewed anonymously by two 
 other students during the online laboratory session. You will also review 
 two submissions from other students using a common rubric and the solution
  discussed during the laboratory.\n\n----------------------------------\nP
 roblem 1 — Sampling Distributions\n----------------------------------\n\
 nWe observe the ages of a sample of 10 students:\n\n[(19\, 23\, 20\, 28\, 
 20\, 34\, 23\, 26\, 25\, 29)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_image
 s/%252819%252C%252023%252C%252020%252C%252028%252C%252020%252C%252034%252C
 %252023%252C%252026%252C%252025%252C%252029%2529)\n\nWe consider a hypothe
 tical infinite population in which age follows a Poisson distribution with
  unknown expectation [\\lambda] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_ima
 ges/%255Clambda). The parameter [\\lambda] (https://um6p.instructure.com/e
 quation_images/%255Clambda) is the parameter of interest.\n\nFour students
  propose different estimators of this parameter:\n\n[\\overline{\\mu}=\\fr
 ac{1}{10}\\sum_{i=1}^{10}X_i] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_image
 s/%255Coverline%257B%255Cmu%257D%253D%255Cfrac%257B1%257D%257B10%257D%255C
 sum_%257Bi%253D1%257D%255E%257B10%257DX_i)\n\n[\\widetilde{\\mu}=\\frac{1}
 {8}\\sum_{i=2}^{9}X_{(i)}] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/%
 255Cwidetilde%257B%255Cmu%257D%253D%255Cfrac%257B1%257D%257B8%257D%255Csum
 _%257Bi%253D2%257D%255E%257B9%257DX_%257B%2528i%2529%257D)\n\n[\\widehat{\
 \mu}=X_{(5)}] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/%255Cwidehat%2
 57B%255Cmu%257D%253DX_%257B%25285%2529%257D)\n\n[\\mu_{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2
 }\\left(X_{(1)}+X_{(10)}\\right)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_i
 mages/%255Cmu_%257B%255Ccirc%257D%253D%255Cfrac%257B1%257D%257B2%257D%255C
 left%2528X_%257B%25281%2529%257D%252BX_%257B%252810%2529%257D%255Cright%25
 29)\n\nwhere [X_{(1)}\\leq X_{(2)}\\leq\\cdots\\leq X_{(10)}] (https://um6
 p.instructure.com/equation_images/X_%257B%25281%2529%257D%255Cleq%2520X_%2
 57B%25282%2529%257D%255Cleq%255Ccdots%255Cleq%2520X_%257B%252810%2529%257D
 ) denote the ordered observations.\n\n* Compute the four estimates from th
 e observed sample.\n\n* Suppose now that [\\lambda=25] (https://um6p.instr
 ucture.com/equation_images/%255Clambda%253D25). Using R\, generate 1000 sa
 mples of size 10 from this population. For each sample\, compute the four 
 estimators.\n\n* Represent the four resulting sampling distributions using
  histograms. For each estimator\, report the mean and standard deviation o
 f its simulated sampling distribution.\n\n* On each histogram\, indicate t
 he value of the corresponding estimator obtained from the observed sample 
 in part (a).\n\n* For each estimator\, compute the proportion of simulated
  values that are smaller than or equal to the value obtained from the obse
 rved sample.\n\n* Based on your simulations\, discuss whether the observed
  sample provides evidence that the students are younger than 25 years on a
 verage. Compare the conclusions obtained from the four estimators.\n\n----
 ----------------------------------------------------\nProblem 2 — Readin
 g a Sequential Data Generating Process\n----------------------------------
 ----------------------\n\nConsider the following R program:\n\nn <- 100\n\
 nX <- numeric(n)\nY <- numeric(n)\n\nX[1] <- rnorm(1\, 2\, 1)\nY[1] <- 1 +
  2*X[1] + rnorm(1\, 0\, 1)\n\nfor (t in 2:n) {\nX[t] <- 0.5*X[t-1] + rnorm
 (1\, 0\, 1)\nY[t] <- 1 + 2*X[t] + 0.5*Y[t-1] + rnorm(1\, 0\, 1)\n}\n\n* De
 scribe in words how the first three pairs [(X_1\,Y_1)\,(X_2\,Y_2)\,(X_3\,Y
 _3)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/%2528X_1%252CY_1%2529%2
 52C%2528X_2%252CY_2%2529%252C%2528X_3%252CY_3%2529) are generated. Be expl
 icit about the order of generation.\n\n* Write the marginal–conditional 
 decomposition of the joint distribution of [(X_1\,Y_1\,X_2\,Y_2\,X_3\,Y_3)
 ] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/%2528X_1%252CY_1%252CX_2%2
 52CY_2%252CX_3%252CY_3%2529).\n\n* When [Y_t] (https://um6p.instructure.co
 m/equation_images/Y_t) is generated\, which variables have already been ge
 nerated? Which of these variables actually enter the mechanism generating 
 [Y_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Y_t)?\n\n* Run the pro
 gram in R. Produce one graph of [X_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equati
 on_images/X_t) against [t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/t
 ) and one graph of [Y_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Y_t
 ) against [t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/t).\n\n* Modif
 y the program so that [Y_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/
 Y_t) depends on [X_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X_t) b
 ut no longer directly on [Y_{t-1}] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_
 images/Y_%257Bt-1%257D). Give both the modified R code and the conditional
  distribution used to generate [Y_t] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equatio
 n_images/Y_t).\n\n------------------------------------------------\nProble
 m 3 — Constructing a DGP with Peer Effects\n----------------------------
 --------------------\n\nWe want to construct a Data Generating Process for
  the grade obtained by a student in the econometrics examination\, [Ec_t] 
 (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Ec_t). The main explanatory 
 variable is the student's mathematics grade\, [Ma_t] (https://um6p.instruc
 ture.com/equation_images/Ma_t).\n\nIndividual performance may also depend 
 on the quality of the cohort to which the student belongs. The theoretical
  economic model is\n\n[Ec_t^*=\\beta_1+\\beta_2Ma_t^*+\\beta_3GMa_t^*] (ht
 tps://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Ec_t%255E%257B%252A%257D%253D%2
 55Cbeta_1%252B%255Cbeta_2Ma_t%255E%257B%252A%257D%252B%255Cbeta_3GMa_t%255
 E%257B%252A%257D)\n\nwhere [GMa_t^*] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equatio
 n_images/GMa_t%255E%257B%252A%257D) is the average mathematics grade of al
 l students belonging to the same cohort as student [t] (https://um6p.instr
 ucture.com/equation_images/t).\n\nSuppose there are 3 cohorts of 20 studen
 ts each. Generate each mathematics grade from a uniform distribution betwe
 en 0 and 20. Use\n\n[\\beta_1=0.5\,\\qquad\\beta_2=0.9\,\\qquad\\beta_3=0.
 05] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/%255Cbeta_1%253D0.5%252C
 %255Cqquad%255Cbeta_2%253D0.9%252C%255Cqquad%255Cbeta_3%253D0.05)\n\nand a
 ssume that the statistical error follows [N(0\,0.1)] (https://um6p.instruc
 ture.com/equation_images/N%25280%252C0.1%2529)\, where the second paramete
 r denotes the variance.\n\n* Explain the order in which the variables must
  be generated. In particular\, explain why the mathematics grades of the c
 ohort must be available before its econometrics grades can be generated.\n
 \n* Describe the complete Data Generating Process step by step in words.\n
 \n* Write R code that generates one sample containing the 60 students\, th
 eir cohort\, their mathematics grade\, their cohort-average mathematics gr
 ade\, and their econometrics grade.\n\n* Produce a scatterplot of the econ
 ometrics grade against the mathematics grade\, distinguishing the three co
 horts.\n\n* Explain why the data cannot in general be generated in the ord
 er [Ma_1\\rightarrow Ec_1\\rightarrow Ma_2\\rightarrow Ec_2\\rightarrow\\c
 dots] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Ma_1%255Crightarrow%25
 20Ec_1%255Crightarrow%2520Ma_2%255Crightarrow%2520Ec_2%255Crightarrow%255C
 cdots). What information required to generate [Ec_1] (https://um6p.instruc
 ture.com/equation_images/Ec_1) would still be missing?\n\n----------------
 -----------------------------------\nProblem 4 — Conditional Variance an
 d Transformation\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nC
 onsider the following Data Generating Process:\n\n[X\\sim U(1\,10)] (https
 ://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X%255Csim%2520U%25281%252C10%2529)
 \n\n[Y\\mid X=x\\sim N(2x\,0.25x^2)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equatio
 n_images/Y%255Cmid%2520X%253Dx%255Csim%2520N%25282x%252C0.25x%255E2%2529)\
 n\nAs throughout the course\, the second parameter of the normal distribut
 ion denotes the variance.\n\n* Give [\\mathrm{E}(Y\\mid X=x)] (https://um6
 p.instructure.com/equation_images/%255Cmathrm%257BE%257D%2528Y%255Cmid%252
 0X%253Dx%2529) and [\\mathrm{Var}(Y\\mid X=x)] (https://um6p.instructure.c
 om/equation_images/%255Cmathrm%257BVar%257D%2528Y%255Cmid%2520X%253Dx%2529
 ).\n\n* Is this DGP homoskedastic or heteroskedastic? Justify your answer.
 \n\n* Generate 500 observations from this DGP in R and plot [Y] (https://u
 m6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Y) against [X] (https://um6p.instructu
 re.com/equation_images/X).\n\nNow define a transformed dependent variable\
 n\n[Z=\\frac{Y}{X}] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Z%253D%2
 55Cfrac%257BY%257D%257BX%257D).\n\n* Derive the conditional distribution o
 f [Z\\mid X=x] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Z%255Cmid%252
 0X%253Dx).\n\n* Give [\\mathrm{Var}(Z\\mid X=x)] (https://um6p.instructure
 .com/equation_images/%255Cmathrm%257BVar%257D%2528Z%255Cmid%2520X%253Dx%25
 29) and determine whether the transformed DGP is homoskedastic or heterosk
 edastic.\n\n* Using the same simulated sample as in part (c)\, construct [
 Z=Y/X] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Z%253DY%252FX) and pl
 ot [Z] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Z) against [X] (https
 ://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X).\n\n* Compare the two graphs. E
 xplain what the transformation has changed in the conditional distribution
  of the dependent variable.\n\n-------------------------------------------
 ---------\nProblem 5 — What Changes and What Remains Invariant?\n-------
 ---------------------------------------------\n\nConsider the following th
 ree Data Generating Processes:\n\nMarginal generation of X\n\nConditional 
 generation of Y\n\nDGP 1\n\n[X\\sim N(1\,1)] (https://um6p.instructure.com
 /equation_images/X%255Csim%2520N%25281%252C1%2529)\n\n[Y\\mid X=x\\sim N(2
 +3x\,1)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/Y%255Cmid%2520X%253
 Dx%255Csim%2520N%25282%252B3x%252C1%2529)\n\nDGP 2\n\n[X\\sim N(4\,1)] (ht
 tps://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X%255Csim%2520N%25284%252C1%252
 9)\n\n[Y\\mid X=x\\sim N(2+3x\,1)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_
 images/Y%255Cmid%2520X%253Dx%255Csim%2520N%25282%252B3x%252C1%2529)\n\nDGP
  3\n\n[X\\sim N(4\,1)] (https://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X%255
 Csim%2520N%25284%252C1%2529)\n\n[Y\\mid X=x\\sim N(2+5x\,1)] (https://um6p
 .instructure.com/equation_images/Y%255Cmid%2520X%253Dx%255Csim%2520N%25282
 %252B5x%252C1%2529)\n\n* Write the theoretical economic relationship assoc
 iated with each DGP.\n\n* Generate 100 observations from each DGP in R. Re
 present the three samples graphically using the same limits for the horizo
 ntal and vertical axes.\n\n* Compare DGP 1 and DGP 2. Which part of the ma
 rginal–conditional decomposition changes? Which part remains invariant?\
 n\n* Compare DGP 2 and DGP 3. Which part changes? Which part remains invar
 iant?\n\n* For each comparison\, state whether the theoretical economic re
 lationship has changed.\n\n* Explain in your own words why two populations
  may have very different distributions of the explanatory variable [X] (ht
 tps://um6p.instructure.com/equation_images/X) while sharing the same theor
 etical economic relationship between [X^*] (https://um6p.instructure.com/e
 quation_images/X%255E%257B%252A%257D) and [Y^*] (https://um6p.instructure.
 com/equation_images/Y%255E%257B%252A%257D).\n\nBefore submitting\n--------
 ---------\n\nYour PDF should contain:\n\n* your answers to all five proble
 ms\;\n\n* all requested R code\;\n\n* all requested numerical results\;\n\
 n* all requested graphs\;\n\n* brief explanations where interpretation is 
 requested.\n\nSubmit one PDF file only.
